1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,432
The school asks you to prepare for posterior, high-lateral and LBBB-
related findings, then integrate atypical or incomplete presentations.
This workshop makes those tasks explicit.

2
00:00:12,432 --> 00:00:22,296
Use the earlier lead placement and rhythm framework. We now add the
measurements and limitations that stop a confident but incorrect
interpretation.

3
00:00:22,296 --> 00:00:31,752
A pattern can justify urgent concern without proving one culprit
artery. A negative special rule also cannot make an unstable patient
safe.

4
00:00:31,752 --> 00:00:43,296
Describe what is present, how reliable it is and what action follows
through the actual regional pathway. These original exercises are not
school examination strips or answers.

5
00:00:43,296 --> 00:00:57,264
Ischemia may present with pressure, breathlessness, weakness, nausea
or syncope rather than a complete classic story. An incomplete history
is a reason to assess, not a reason to exclude.

6
00:00:57,264 --> 00:01:06,792
Describe the actual symptoms and time course. The current chest-pain
approach discourages using atypical as a dismissive shortcut.

7
00:01:06,792 --> 00:01:18,720
Look for poor output, heart failure and dangerous alternatives while
obtaining the useful ECG. No individual examination finding proves or
excludes coronary ischemia.

8
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:32,280
Ask what would change the immediate plan: shock, ongoing symptoms,
electrical instability or a concerning evolving trace. Do not spend
the encounter collecting risk factors while function fails.

9
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:41,808
Confirm the patient, time and symptom or treatment state. A
technically valid old tracing is not necessarily the current
physiological picture.

10
00:01:41,808 --> 00:01:52,488
Use actual landmarks and correct lead identity. Misplaced precordial
or reversed limb electrodes can create misleading territory and
conduction appearances.

11
00:01:52,488 --> 00:02:03,792
Reduce correctable artifact and inspect the baseline before measuring
a tiny J-point difference. A machine interpretation is a prompt for
review, not a quality guarantee.

12
00:02:03,792 --> 00:02:13,392
Keep all useful recordings with times and placement labels. Never
discard the standard trace merely because supplemental leads have been
acquired.

13
00:02:13,392 --> 00:02:24,144
At standard gain, one millimeter corresponds to zero point one
millivolt. Check the actual gain and paper speed before using
remembered grid dimensions.

14
00:02:24,144 --> 00:02:34,080
Locate QRS onset and the J point at the end of QRS. Baseline drift and
an indistinct end can make a small measurement unreliable.

15
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:45,384
The universal-definition reference uses QRS onset for J-point
comparison in the acute setting. Do not accidentally measure the
T-wave peak or use a moving baseline as the reference.

16
00:02:45,384 --> 00:02:55,128
State the measured finding and uncertainty. A threshold only helps
when the calibration, anatomy and clinical setting match the rule
being used.

17
00:02:55,128 --> 00:03:05,016
Group the views by anatomy rather than their printed adjacency. Two
boxes next to each other on the page are not automatically contiguous
leads.

18
00:03:05,016 --> 00:03:17,496
Inferior Two, Three and a V F are a familiar group. One and a V L
examine high lateral regions, with V Five and V Six providing related
lateral views.

19
00:03:17,496 --> 00:03:28,512
Precordial territories overlap; use a consistent learned map without
pretending each electrode sees one isolated wall or uniquely
identifies an artery.

20
00:03:28,512 --> 00:03:39,672
Supplemental leads change the view and must be labelled. A trace
acquired after moving electrodes cannot be compared as if every lead
position remained standard.

21
00:03:39,672 --> 00:03:50,784
For conventional criteria, new or presumed-new elevation must be
assessed in anatomically contiguous leads with the correct context,
not in one isolated spike.

22
00:03:50,784 --> 00:04:02,472
The usual one-millimeter cutoff does not apply unchanged to V Two and
V Three. In men forty or older the cutoff there is two millimeters,
and under forty it is two point five.

23
00:04:02,472 --> 00:04:15,264
For women, the cited V Two and V Three cutoff is one point five
millimeters. The complete rule still includes lead relationships and
relevant conduction or hypertrophy context.

24
00:04:15,264 --> 00:04:26,400
Lesser changes can still be important with symptoms or reciprocal
findings. Failure to meet conventional elevation criteria does not
exclude acute coronary occlusion.

25
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:34,944
State the abnormal leads and the kind of change before naming a
territory. This keeps the interpretation traceable to the evidence.

26
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:45,096
Reciprocal depression can support concern but is not required in every
true event. No mirror change does not make a concerning elevation
harmless.

27
00:04:45,096 --> 00:04:55,728
Coronary anatomy varies and regional views overlap. A territory is not
proof of a particular culprit vessel or the exact size and age of
infarction.

28
00:04:55,728 --> 00:05:07,224
Communicate the pattern and patient-linked urgency through the actual
pathway. Do not delay the alert while trying to prove anatomy that
only definitive investigation can establish.

29
00:05:07,224 --> 00:05:17,088
The standard tracing has limited direct posterior views. Horizontal
depression in V One through V Three can be a reciprocal clue to
posterior injury.

30
00:05:17,088 --> 00:05:26,688
A prominent broad R wave, an R-to-S ratio above one in V Two and
upright anterior T waves can support the pattern in the appropriate
context.

31
00:05:26,688 --> 00:05:37,656
These findings are not specific alone and may not all be present
early. Do not require a complete memorized triad before taking ongoing
ischemic symptoms seriously.

32
00:05:37,656 --> 00:05:50,928
Acquire indicated trained posterior views and seek appropriate urgent
interpretation/pathway support. The absence of ordinary anterior
elevation is not a reassuring conclusion.

33
00:05:50,928 --> 00:06:03,384
Use the trained supplemental placement method. V Seven lies at the
left posterior axillary line, V Eight at the left midscapular line and
V Nine at the left paraspinal border.

34
00:06:03,384 --> 00:06:12,600
Maintain the horizontal posterior level specified by the reference and
actual protocol, rather than following the changing scapular tip down
the back.

35
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:24,528
If existing cables are repositioned, label the new positions clearly
on the actual recording. Do not leave the receiver to interpret
posterior positions as standard V Four to V Six.

36
00:06:24,528 --> 00:06:36,048
Preserve the standard ECG and both acquisition times. Obtain useful
additional views without abandoning treatment of instability or
delaying necessary communication.

37
00:06:36,048 --> 00:06:50,088
The cited universal-definition section gives a half-millimeter
posterior elevation cutoff, with one millimeter used for men younger
than forty. These are supplemental-view criteria, not a new rule for
every standard lead.

38
00:06:50,088 --> 00:07:00,288
A small signal demands a reliable baseline, correct placement and
calibration. Do not manufacture precision from a moving or poor-
quality tracing.

39
00:07:00,288 --> 00:07:12,096
The downloaded twenty-twenty-two table prints a less-than-or-equal
sign in this posterior item. It conflicts with the threshold concept
and is not taught as a maximum allowed elevation.

40
00:07:12,096 --> 00:07:24,696
The local notification and destination process still applies. High
concern with ongoing symptoms or instability deserves escalation even
when a supplemental trace is not definitive.

41
00:07:24,696 --> 00:07:33,384
Posterior signals may be small and disease evolves. A nondiagnostic
extra recording is not a universal rule-out test.

42
00:07:33,384 --> 00:07:44,928
Check placement, quality and acquisition timing, then compare genuine
serial changes. Do not repeatedly move electrodes and call every
difference physiological evolution.

43
00:07:44,928 --> 00:07:58,224
Anterior depression can have other explanations, including wider
ischemia or conduction-related changes. Describe the observations and
alternatives rather than declaring posterior infarction from one sign.

44
00:07:58,224 --> 00:08:08,352
Continue appropriate patient support and timely expert review.
Repeating tests must not become a reason to delay a necessary pre-
alert or conveyance.

45
00:08:08,352 --> 00:08:18,336
The school includes high-lateral patterns in its advanced list.
Elevation in One and a V L can meet ordinary contiguous-lead STEMI
criteria.

46
00:08:18,336 --> 00:08:27,744
Review related lateral views and possible inferior reciprocal
depression. These support localization but do not independently prove
the culprit artery.

47
00:08:27,744 --> 00:08:39,912
Subtle isolated findings need quality and clinical correlation, not a
forced equivalent label. Do not ignore a small but concerning change
simply because another lead looks normal.

48
00:08:39,912 --> 00:08:52,200
Use the actual alert pathway and communicate the abnormal views,
symptom state and stability. Classification should help timely care,
not become an argument that delays it.

49
00:08:52,200 --> 00:09:02,064
A wide QRS means ventricular activation takes longer. It does not
alone prove LBBB, RBBB or ventricular tachycardia.

50
00:09:02,064 --> 00:09:13,872
Pacing, pre-excitation, electrolyte/drug effects and other conduction
disease can also produce a broad complex. Artifact may further confuse
the apparent width.

51
00:09:13,872 --> 00:09:23,928
Use the measured duration, morphology, rhythm and atrioventricular
relationships. Check the whole lead set rather than one memorable
shape.

52
00:09:23,928 --> 00:09:33,696
Assess pulse and perfusion promptly. An unstable wide-complex patient
needs the appropriate urgent pathway while the interpretation is
refined.

53
00:09:33,696 --> 00:09:43,200
The cited adult complete-RBBB definition includes a QRS of at least
one hundred twenty milliseconds. The duration alone is insufficient.

54
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:54,792
Look for the specified terminal R-prime patterns in V One or V Two,
with a broad terminal S in One and V Six. Recognize that real
morphology has variants.

55
00:09:54,792 --> 00:10:05,472
The full definition also considers R-peak timing. Do not reduce it to
a cartoon of rabbit ears or infer a diagnosis from one misplaced
right-precordial lead.

56
00:10:05,472 --> 00:10:18,504
RBBB is a conduction description, not automatic infarction, embolism
or atrioventricular block. New symptoms and perfusion changes still
need an urgent clinical explanation.

57
00:10:18,504 --> 00:10:29,832
The cited adult complete-LBBB definition begins with QRS at least one
hundred twenty milliseconds and characteristic broad, notched or
slurred lateral R waves.

58
00:10:29,832 --> 00:10:40,512
The complete morphology includes absent Q waves in One, V Five and V
Six, with the stated a V L exception, and delayed lateral R-peak
timing.

59
00:10:40,512 --> 00:10:51,312
ST and T usually oppose the dominant QRS direction. These secondary
changes make ordinary ischemia reading harder; not every elevation is
primary injury.

60
00:10:51,312 --> 00:11:05,976
Use the whole pattern and clinical context rather than the machine
label alone. Alternative LBBB definitions exist for specialist
purposes, so do not import a CRT threshold into this basic recognition
task.

61
00:11:05,976 --> 00:11:16,584
Atrioventricular block describes impaired conduction from atria toward
ventricles. Bundle-branch block describes delayed spread within the
ventricular conduction system.

62
00:11:16,584 --> 00:11:27,816
They can coexist, but a wide QRS does not establish complete AV block.
Inspect atrial activity and its relationship to conducted ventricular
beats.

63
00:11:27,816 --> 00:11:40,248
Likewise, a two-to-one pattern cannot always be called Mobitz Two from
one strip. Describe the observed relationship and seek appropriate
review without delaying unstable-patient care.

64
00:11:40,248 --> 00:11:51,528
The practical endpoint is a useful description of rate, rhythm,
conduction and perfusion. A more precise label is valuable only when
it improves the decision.

65
00:11:51,528 --> 00:12:01,200
Concordance means that the ST displacement and dominant QRS point in
the same direction. A positive QRS with elevation is one example.

66
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:11,784
Discordance means opposite directions. In LBBB, some discordant ST-T
change is expected because activation and repolarization have changed.

67
00:12:11,784 --> 00:12:21,072
Expected does not mean unlimited. The amount relative to the preceding
complex and the clinical context can make a discordant finding
concerning.

68
00:12:21,072 --> 00:12:31,920
Use the schematic to understand direction, then measure on a real
calibrated trace. A teaching drawing is not enough to diagnose LBBB or
acute occlusion.

69
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:42,936
The school lists LBBB among advanced equivalent concerns. The
important correction is that new or presumed-new LBBB alone is not
diagnostic of acute MI.

70
00:12:42,936 --> 00:12:55,032
A previous tracing can help establish whether the pattern changed, but
obtaining it must not delay urgent care. If no old trace exists, say
that newness is unknown.

71
00:12:55,032 --> 00:13:07,032
Assess ongoing ischemic symptoms, perfusion and specific concerning ST
relationships. LBBB can indicate underlying structural disease even
without acute occlusion.

72
00:13:07,032 --> 00:13:18,504
Urgently escalate an unstable or persistently symptomatic patient
through the actual pathway. A negative special score is not a
discharge or non-conveyance certificate.

73
00:13:18,504 --> 00:13:26,904
The cited framework treats concordant elevation of at least one
millimeter in a lead with a positive QRS as an important finding.

74
00:13:26,904 --> 00:13:37,728
It also identifies concordant depression of at least one millimeter in
V One to V Three. Confirm direction and baseline rather than merely
seeing any depression.

75
00:13:37,728 --> 00:13:48,216
These rules are discussed for LBBB or the appropriate ventricular-
paced context. They are not a universal score for every conduction
pattern or wide tachycardia.

76
00:13:48,216 --> 00:13:58,200
Positive findings support urgent concern; their absence does not
exclude occlusion. Continue patient-linked assessment and timely
expert review.

77
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:08,520
Original Sgarbossa assigns five points for concordant elevation, three
for concordant anterior depression and two for the absolute
discordant-elevation item.

78
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:18,984
A score at least three is positive in the cited framework. You do not
need all three items; the two-point item alone is not the same as
reaching the score threshold.

79
00:14:18,984 --> 00:14:29,736
The absolute five-millimeter discordant item has limitations when the
preceding QRS is very large or small. This motivates the proportional
modification.

80
00:14:29,736 --> 00:14:39,024
Do not combine original points with modified items into a new hybrid
score. State which framework you used and seek review when uncertain.

81
00:14:39,024 --> 00:14:47,952
Smith-modified Sgarbossa keeps the concordant findings and replaces
the absolute discordant-elevation item with a proportional
measurement.

82
00:14:47,952 --> 00:14:57,240
In the cited table, the J-point elevation must be at least one
millimeter and at least one quarter of the preceding S-wave depth.

83
00:14:57,240 --> 00:15:07,584
Use the magnitude of the S-wave depth as the denominator, measured
from the proper reference. It is not a T-wave ratio or the height of
the tallest R elsewhere.

84
00:15:07,584 --> 00:15:20,448
The modified rule is positive if any qualifying item is present, not
by adding the original weights. It supports an urgent interpretation
pathway, not independent proof of one artery.

85
00:15:20,448 --> 00:15:30,552
Assume a calibrated interpretable LBBB-context lead has a preceding S
wave twelve millimeters deep and J-point elevation four millimeters
high.

86
00:15:30,552 --> 00:15:41,592
Divide four by twelve: the magnitude is about zero point three three,
or thirty-three percent. Elevation also exceeds the one-millimeter
minimum.

87
00:15:41,592 --> 00:15:53,184
That meets the proportional teaching item in this assumed example. If
the S depth were twenty-four with the same four-millimeter elevation,
the proportion would be about seventeen percent instead.

88
00:15:53,184 --> 00:16:05,280
A real patient decision still requires the right context, whole trace,
symptoms and pathway. The second example not meeting one item does not
rule out coronary occlusion.

89
00:16:05,280 --> 00:16:17,424
Before using the rule, verify the trace quality and the actual
conduction context. Mislabelled pacing, artifact or an unclear
baseline can invalidate the calculation.

90
00:16:17,424 --> 00:16:27,648
The original criteria are specific but not sensitive enough to exclude
acute occlusion. A negative result does not explain away ongoing
symptoms or shock.

91
00:16:27,648 --> 00:16:37,920
Do not use an exact percentage as a substitute for judgment when
measurements are uncertain. State the uncertainty and obtain a timely
expert over-read.

92
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:49,176
Keep treating the immediate patient threat and follow the real
notification pathway. A classroom rule cannot authorize automatic
bypass, thrombolysis or a new medication.

93
00:16:49,176 --> 00:17:00,576
Determine whether pacing is atrial, ventricular or another
configuration and whether the trace is interpretable. A spike alone
does not describe ventricular activation.

94
00:17:00,576 --> 00:17:10,008
Check actual capture and perfusion through the patient assessment.
Electrical-looking capture does not by itself prove effective
mechanical output.

95
00:17:10,008 --> 00:17:20,856
The cited ischemia framework includes an appropriate ventricular-paced
context, but not every paced trace is identical to LBBB or suitable
for automatic scoring.

96
00:17:20,856 --> 00:17:31,104
Describe the device context, symptoms and concerning findings, then
seek appropriate review. Pacing does not make acute coronary disease
impossible.

97
00:17:31,104 --> 00:17:43,608
Hyperacute T changes can be early ischemic findings, but potassium
disturbance and hypertrophy can also affect T waves. No tall T wave is
automatically one diagnosis.

98
00:17:43,608 --> 00:17:55,776
The de Winter description combines upsloping precordial ST depression
with prominent symmetric T waves. It can indicate serious coronary
occlusion without ordinary precordial elevation.

99
00:17:55,776 --> 00:18:07,224
Wellens is a clinical pattern with characteristic anterior biphasic or
deep symmetric T changes and a relevant recent symptom history, not a
label for any inverted T.

100
00:18:07,224 --> 00:18:18,096
Describe the real findings and escalate the risk through the actual
pathway. Do not add a provocative exercise test or declare a specific
vessel from a cartoon.

101
00:18:18,096 --> 00:18:29,136
Widespread depression with a V R elevation can represent high-risk
diffuse subendocardial ischemia. It is not proof that every patient
has an acute left-main occlusion.

102
00:18:29,136 --> 00:18:40,752
Severe coronary disease and systemic supply-demand mismatch can both
contribute. Hemorrhage, hypoxia or a compensatory fast rate may be
central to the current problem.

103
00:18:40,752 --> 00:18:51,912
Treat the actual reversible threats and support perfusion while
escalating ischemic concern. Do not reflexively slow a bleeding
patient because the tracing has a fast rate.

104
00:18:51,912 --> 00:19:02,904
Communicate the pattern, patient state and response rather than a
falsely certain anatomy. The receiving team needs both the ECG and the
physiological explanation.

105
00:19:02,904 --> 00:19:14,424
Repeat through the indicated pathway when symptoms recur, physiology
changes or concern remains. A single nondiagnostic ECG is not the
final story.

106
00:19:14,424 --> 00:19:24,528
Use comparable placement and note supplemental positions. A difference
caused by moving electrodes must not be silently called dynamic
ischemia.

107
00:19:24,528 --> 00:19:34,080
Preserve acquisition time, symptom state and intervention sequence. An
earlier transient finding may remain important after it resolves.

108
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:44,976
Communicate concern promptly and seek an expert over-read when needed.
Do not wait for every label to become certain while an unstable
patient deteriorates.

109
00:19:44,976 --> 00:19:55,344
Maintain primary airway, breathing and circulation care while
interpreting. A beautiful trace is not useful if obtaining it
displaced needed support.

110
00:19:55,344 --> 00:20:06,096
Ask whether the rhythm or ischemia causes the low perfusion,
contributes to it or reflects compensation for another process. The
same rate can mean different things.

111
00:20:06,096 --> 00:20:15,528
A traumatic or bleeding patient can also have coronary disease. Do not
let the cardiac label hide uncontrolled hemorrhage or an airway
problem.

112
00:20:15,528 --> 00:20:25,608
Escalate through the actual regional system with an accurate
description of both threats. You do not need angiographic certainty to
request needed capability.

113
00:20:25,608 --> 00:20:39,192
A sixty-nine-year-old at a garden exhibition develops sudden
breathlessness and sweating without a classic pain description. Assess
function and obtain a useful ECG rather than dismissing the
presentation.

114
00:20:39,192 --> 00:20:52,272
Assume the reliable tracing shows horizontal depression in V One to V
Three with supportive R and T features. State posterior ischemic
concern without calling the finding specific proof.

115
00:20:52,272 --> 00:21:04,512
Obtain indicated trained supplemental views while assessing cardiac
failure, embolism and other dangerous causes. Continue support and
preserve the original trace and times.

116
00:21:04,512 --> 00:21:16,824
If the extra trace remains nondiagnostic but symptoms or instability
persist, escalate the concern and seek review. A negative extra view
is not permission to stop caring.

117
00:21:16,824 --> 00:21:28,800
A sixty-three-year-old at a ferry terminal has weakness, chest
pressure and poor perfusion. The ECG has the adult LBBB morphology,
but no prior tracing is available.

118
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:39,000
Do not label it definitely new, and do not diagnose MI from LBBB
alone. Review the whole trace and the specific concerning ST
relationships.

119
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:49,848
Use the referenced criteria carefully, state any measurement
uncertainty and consider other contributors to shock. A negative
result would not make this patient stable.

120
00:21:49,848 --> 00:22:02,760
Continue appropriate support and urgent notification/consultation
through the actual pathway. Report LBBB of unknown age with the
observed ischemic concern and current physiology.

121
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:12,888
The school target is a complete pre-alert within sixty seconds. Start
with the unit, patient and immediate threat so the receiver can
allocate attention.

122
00:22:12,888 --> 00:22:26,112
Give symptom timing, current perfusion and the actual abnormal leads
or conduction context. State posterior concern or LBBB of unknown age
rather than invented certainty.

123
00:22:26,112 --> 00:22:35,400
Report treatment already given and response, including adverse change.
Separate a possible intervention from one actually administered.

124
00:22:35,400 --> 00:22:46,224
Finish with ETA, capability required and confirmation of the plan. The
clock tests organized communication, not speed at the expense of the
key risk.

125
00:22:46,224 --> 00:22:56,592
You should now explain why anterior depression may raise posterior
concern, where the additional views belong and why high lateral is not
always an equivalent.

126
00:22:56,592 --> 00:23:06,144
Distinguish adult RBBB and LBBB using duration and complete
morphology, and distinguish bundle disease from atrioventricular
block.

127
00:23:06,144 --> 00:23:15,576
Reconstruct concordance, original weights and the proportional
modification, including the S-wave denominator and the limits of a
negative result.

128
00:23:15,576 --> 00:23:27,960
Finally connect the finding to the current patient, serial evidence
and an actionable pre-alert. The integrated workshop next combines
these decisions with ROSC deterioration and trauma.
